MAKE(1)                        P32W Unix                         MAKE(1)

NAME
       make - keep programs up to date

SYNOPSIS
       make [-f file] [-C dir] [-n] [-s] [-k] [-i] [-p]
            [name=value ...] [target ...]

DESCRIPTION
       make rebuilds files that are older than the files they are
       made from, following the rules in a makefile: Makefile (or
       makefile) in the current directory, or the file named with
       -f. With no target it makes the first target in the
       makefile.

       A target is remade when it does not exist, when any of its
       prerequisites is newer than it, or when a prerequisite was
       just remade. Prerequisites are made first, recursively.

MAKEFILES
       A makefile holds variables, rules and commands:

       CFLAGS = -w
              a variable, used as $(CFLAGS) or ${CFLAGS}; a one-letter
              name also as $X. := expands the value at once, +=
              appends to it, ?= sets it only if it is not set. A
              name=value on the command line overrides the makefile;
              the environment supplies a default.
       prog: main.o util.o
       <tab>cc -o prog main.o util.o
              a rule: the target, a colon, its prerequisites, then
              the commands, each on a line starting with a tab. A
              command may also follow a ; on the rule's line.
       %.o: %.c
              a pattern rule: % matches the same text (the stem) on
              both sides. The old suffix forms .c.o: and .c: work
              too.
       .PHONY: clean install
              targets that are not files: always made when asked.
       include file
              read another makefile here.

       A \ at the end of a line continues it; # starts a comment.
       $(NAME:.c=.o) is NAME with each word's suffix .c replaced by
       .o.

       In commands, $@ is the target, $< the first prerequisite, $^
       all of them, $? those newer than the target, $* the stem; $$
       is a $. Each command runs in sh -c, or by itself if it has
       nothing in it for sh to do, one at a time; a command starting
       with @ is not shown first, and one starting with - may fail
       without stopping make.

       Built in: CC=cc, AS=as, AR=ar, CFLAGS, LDFLAGS and LDLIBS
       (empty), and the rules
              %.o: %.c   $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c $<
              %.o: %.s   $(AS) -o $@ $<
              %:   %.c   $(CC) $(CFLAGS) $(LDFLAGS) -o $@ $< $(LDLIBS)
       so a one-file program needs no makefile: make hello builds
       hello from hello.c.

OPTIONS
       -f file
              read file instead of Makefile (- reads the standard
              input).
       -C dir change to dir first.
       -n     show the commands that would run, and run none.
       -s     run the commands without showing them.
       -k     after a failure, go on with targets that do not depend
              on the one that failed.
       -i     ignore every command's failure.
       -p     print the variables and rules, and make nothing.

EXAMPLES
       make hello                   build hello from hello.c
       make -n                      what would be done
       make CFLAGS=-w prog          with a variable changed

       A small makefile:

              OBJS = main.o list.o
              prog: $(OBJS)
                      cc -o prog $(OBJS)
              $(OBJS): list.h
              clean:
                      rm -f prog $(OBJS)
              .PHONY: clean

SYSTEM SOURCES
       /usr/src holds the sources of P32W Unix with their makefiles,
       and what they build (so the first make there only rebuilds
       what has been edited). The group staff may build there.

       /usr/src/cmd   the commands: make rebuilds those whose source
                      changed, make ls just ls; make install (as
                      root) puts every rebuilt one that differs from
                      the running system into /bin or /etc.
       /usr/src/cc    the C compiler: make, make install, and make
                      test (the new compiler compiles itself; the
                      result must be identical).
       /usr/src/libc  the C library: make and make install (into
                      /usr/lib; programs linked afterwards use it).
       /usr/src/kernel
                      the kernel: make builds unix.bin. No make
                      install yet: only the host can put a kernel
                      on the disk.

EXIT STATUS
       0 when everything is up to date or was remade; 2 after an
       error.

SEE ALSO
       cc(1), sh(1), as(1), ar(1)

BUGS
       Times are compared to the second, so a target made in the same
       second as its source looks up to date; touch the source again.
       There are no parallel builds (-j), no functions ($(wildcard),
       $(shell)), no conditionals (ifeq) and no archive members
       (lib.a(x.o)). A failed command's half-made target is removed.
